# services/data-pipeline's own `.env` (gitignored) -- copy this file to
# `.env` and fill in real values. `Settings` (app/core/config.py) has
# sensible localhost defaults for everything below except `DATABASE_URL`,
# so a local `docker-compose up` stack mostly works without a `.env` at
# all -- this file exists for the values worth being explicit about, not
# an exhaustive dump of every `Settings` field.

# PostgreSQL 16 + TimescaleDB. Required -- no working default (the
# decomposed postgres_host/user/password/db fields fall back to a bare
# `postgresql+asyncpg://postgres:postgres@localhost:5432/ecolens`, which
# only works against this repo's own docker-compose `postgres` service).
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://user:password@host:5432/ecolens

# MLflow tracking server (README.md § MLflow). `docker-compose up mlflow`
# serves this at http://localhost:5000 on the host, or http://mlflow:5000
# from inside another compose service. Port 5000 collides with macOS's
# AirPlay Receiver if you're running MLflow as a bare local process
# instead of via compose -- that shows up as an unexplained 403 on every
# MLflow API call, not a connection error, so it's worth checking
# `System Settings -> General -> AirDrop & Handoff` first if this happens.
MLFLOW_TRACKING_URI=http://localhost:5000
MLFLOW_REGISTRY_MODEL_NAME=lstm_demand

# JWT bearer auth (this service's own token issuer, `POST /v1/auth/token`).
# Change this in any real deployment -- the default in Settings is a
# clearly-fake placeholder, not something to leave running past local dev.
IAM_JWT_SECRET=

# OpenElectricity / BoM (optional -- anonymous/rate-limited access without
# these).
# OE_API_KEY=
# BOM_API_KEY=

# Cloudflare R2 (S3-compatible object storage -- TODO.md's Storage item:
# dashboard static assets + MLflow model-weight artifact backups). All
# four required together for `app.service.object_storage` to use R2 --
# with any of them unset, `Settings.object_storage_configured` is False
# and everything falls back to local MinIO instead. CLOUDFLARESTORAGE_S3_API
# is the endpoint Cloudflare hands you when you create the bucket
# (typically `https://<account-id>.r2.cloudflarestorage.com/<bucket>`);
# the access key id/secret are a separate R2 API token (R2 -> Manage API
# tokens), not your Cloudflare account credentials.
# CLOUDFLARESTORAGE_ACCOUNT_ID=
# CLOUDFLARESTORAGE_S3_API=
# CLOUDFLARESTORAGE_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
# CLOUDFLARESTORAGE_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
